---
description: FastAPI backend rules for FinShield AI
globs: backend/**, app/**, api/**, server/**, **/*.py
---

Backend stack (FinShield AI):
- Python 3.12
- FastAPI
- Pydantic v2
- SQLAlchemy 2.0 (async) with Alembic migrations
- PostgreSQL 16 (Azure Database for PostgreSQL) — no Supabase
- Redis 7 (Azure Cache for Redis) for cache, Celery broker, rate limiting, session state
- Celery for async tasks (notifications, report generation, batch jobs)
- Azure Event Hubs (Kafka-compatible) for transaction streaming
- Socket.IO (python-socketio) for real-time alerts and dashboard updates
- ML: scikit-learn, XGBoost, PyTorch; ONNX Runtime for inference
- Azure Blob Storage for model artifacts, reports, exports
- Azure Key Vault for secrets (via config/env; never hardcode)
- Email (SendGrid/Azure) and SMS (Twilio) for alert notifications — configurable

Backend rules:
- Use clean architecture: routes → services → repositories/models.
- Routes/controllers handle only HTTP/WebSocket concerns.
- Business logic lives in services (fraud_detection, risk_scoring, rules_engine, case, entity, watchlist, analytics, notification, audit).
- Database access via repositories or async SQLAlchemy session; no raw SQL in routes.
- Validation via Pydantic schemas; centralized exception handling.
- Use async I/O for DB, Redis, and external calls where applicable.
- Pagination and consistent response models for list endpoints.
- Support WebSocket/Socket.IO for real-time alerts and transaction feed per API_SPEC.md.

Preferred backend structure (per CLAUDE.md):
- app/api/v1/              (HTTP routes: auth, transactions, fraud_alerts, risk_scoring, rules, cases, entities, models, watchlists, audit, settings, users, webhooks, network, health)
- app/core/                (config, security, permissions, rate_limiter, middleware, exceptions, events)
- app/models/              (SQLAlchemy ORM: user, transaction, fraud_alert, rule, case, entity, risk_score, watchlist, audit_log, ml_model, notification, webhook)
- app/schemas/             (Pydantic request/response)
- app/services/            (business logic)
- app/ml/                  (pipeline, feature_engineering, model_registry, anomaly_detector, fraud_classifier, risk_scorer, behavioral_profiler, network_analyzer, explainability, training/)
- app/rules/               (engine, conditions, actions, templates)
- app/streaming/           (Event Hub consumer, producer, processor, websocket_manager)
- app/integrations/        (azure_ml, azure_storage, azure_keyvault, email_service, sms_service, sanctions_api)
- app/db/                  (session, migrations, seed)

Coding expectations:
- Type hints on all function signatures.
- Docstrings for non-trivial service methods.
- Keep routes thin and declarative.
- Isolate integrations (Azure, email, SMS) behind interfaces; use config/env for credentials.

Do not:
- Put SQL or ORM-heavy logic in route files.
- Put business logic in Pydantic schemas.
- Hardcode database URLs, Redis URLs, Event Hub connection, or API keys; centralize in config.
- Mix unrelated domains in the same service file.
